CPU Scheduling Algorithms (Deep Dive)

This is one of the most important OS interview topics because it answers:

When multiple processes are waiting for the CPU, who gets the CPU first?

Why Do We Need CPU Scheduling?

Suppose Ready Queue contains:

Chrome
VS Code
Spotify
Notepad

CPU can execute only one process per core at a time.

Question:

Who should run first?

OS needs a decision-making mechanism.

That mechanism is:

CPU Scheduling
Definition

CPU Scheduling is the process of selecting a process from the Ready Queue and allocating the CPU to it.

Real-Life Example

Imagine a doctor.

Patients arrive:

Patient A
Patient B
Patient C
Patient D

Doctor can examine only one patient at a time.

A rule is needed.

Examples:

First Come First Serve
Emergency First
Shortest Treatment First

These are like scheduling algorithms.

Goals of CPU Scheduling

A good scheduler tries to:

Maximize CPU Utilization

Keep CPU busy.

Maximize Throughput

Complete more processes per unit time.

Minimize Waiting Time

Reduce time spent in Ready Queue.

Minimize Turnaround Time

Reduce total completion time.

Improve Response Time

Important for interactive systems.

Important Terms First

Before algorithms, learn these.

Arrival Time (AT)

Time when process enters Ready Queue.

Example:

Process	Arrival Time
P1	0
P2	2
P3	4
Burst Time (BT)

CPU time required.

Example:

Process	Burst Time
P1	5 ms
P2	3 ms
P3	2 ms
Completion Time (CT)

Time when process finishes.

Turnaround Time (TAT)

Formula:

Meaning:

Total time process spent in system.

Waiting Time (WT)

Formula:

Meaning:

Time spent waiting in Ready Queue.

Response Time (RT)

Time from arrival until first CPU allocation.

Types of Scheduling

Two major categories.

1. Non-Preemptive Scheduling

Once CPU is given:

Process keeps CPU
until completion or blocking.

OS cannot forcibly take CPU away.

Example:

You start speaking in class.

Teacher lets you finish.

Algorithms:

FCFS
Non-Preemptive SJF
Non-Preemptive Priority
2. Preemptive Scheduling

OS can take CPU away.

Example:

Teacher says:

Your 5 minutes are over.
Next student.

Algorithms:

Round Robin
SRTF
Preemptive Priority
Algorithm 1: FCFS (First Come First Serve)

Simplest scheduling algorithm.

Rule

Process arriving first gets CPU first.

Example:

Process	Burst
P1	5
P2	3
P3	2

Arrival order:

P1 → P2 → P3

Gantt Chart:

0-----5---8--10
| P1 |P2|P3|

Completion Times

Process	CT
P1	5
P2	8
P3	10

Advantages

✔ Simple

✔ Easy to implement

Disadvantages

✖ Convoy Effect

Convoy Effect

Suppose:

Process	Burst
P1	100
P2	2
P3	1

FCFS:

P1 first

Result:

Small processes wait behind giant process.

Inefficient.

Algorithm 2: SJF (Shortest Job First)

Very important interview topic.

Rule

Shortest Burst Time executes first.

Example:

Process	Burst
P1	8
P2	4
P3	2

Execution:

P3 → P2 → P1

Gantt:

0--2----6--------14
 P3 P2      P1

Advantage

✔ Minimum average waiting time (theoretically optimal among non-preemptive algorithms)

Disadvantage

✖ Difficult to know future burst time.

Algorithm 3: SRTF

Shortest Remaining Time First

Preemptive version of SJF.

Rule:

Process with shortest remaining CPU time runs.

Example:

P1 starts.

Suddenly:

Shorter process arrives.

CPU switches immediately.

This uses:

Timer Interrupt
Context Switching

which you learned earlier.

Algorithm 4: Priority Scheduling

Each process receives a priority.

Example:

Process	Priority
P1	1
P2	3
P3	5

Assume:

Lower number = Higher priority

Execution:

P1 → P2 → P3

Real-Life Example

Hospital:

Heart Attack Patient
Cold Patient
Checkup Patient

Emergency first.

Problem: Starvation

Low-priority process may wait forever.

Example:

Every time:

Higher priority process arrives.

Low-priority process never gets CPU.

Solution: Aging

Increase priority gradually.

Example:

Waiting 10 minutes
Priority increases

Eventually process runs.

Algorithm 5: Round Robin (RR)

Most important for interviews and real OS.

Used in:

Time-sharing systems
Interactive systems

Rule

Every process gets a fixed:

Time Quantum

Example:

Quantum = 4 ms

Processes:

Process	Burst
P1	10
P2	5
P3	8

Execution:

P1 (4)
P2 (4)
P3 (4)
P1 (4)
P2 (1)
P3 (4)
P1 (2)

Like sharing turns in a game.

Advantages

✔ Fair

✔ Good response time

✔ Interactive systems

Disadvantages

Too many context switches if quantum is very small.

Time Quantum Selection

Very Important Interview Question.

Small Quantum

More Context Switching
Higher Overhead

Large Quantum

Behaves like FCFS

Need balance.

Algorithm 6: Multilevel Queue Scheduling

Processes divided into queues.

Example:

System Processes
Interactive Processes
Batch Processes

Each queue has its own algorithm.

Example:

System Queue → Priority
User Queue → Round Robin
Algorithm 7: Multilevel Feedback Queue

Most advanced classical scheduler.

Processes can move between queues.

Example:

Queue 1 → Highest Priority
Queue 2 → Medium Priority
Queue 3 → Lowest Priority

CPU-Hungry Process

moves downward.

Interactive Process

stays near top.

Provides:

✔ Flexibility

✔ Good response time

✔ Reduced starvation

